[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 1: White Plays The c3 and e3 Setup  / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Bd3 {White's third option is to develop their light-squared bishop to d3. This is played 22% of the time. Even against Bd3, we'll bring our queen out and attack the b2-pawn. White can play Qc2 now, but our plan then is simply ...c4 and ...Bf5.} 5...Qb6 6.Qb3 {White responds by bringing their queen out and offering a trade. This is the most common response. We'll respond by advancing our c-pawn and forking White's pieces.} 6...c4 7.Qxb6 {White is forced to trade queens. We recapture with the b-pawn.} 7...axb6 8.Bc2 {White moves their bishop back. And now we advance our b-pawn. Our plan is to advance ...b4 (and even ...b3 if allowed) in order to take more space and completely dominate White on the queenside.} 8...b5 9.Na3 {The most resilient move for White here is Na3, attacking the b-pawn. We'll respond by advancing the b-pawn and attacking White's knight.} 9...b4 10.Nb5 {White jumps their knight forward, looking to fork our king and rook. And we'll respond by lifting our rook and attacking White's knight! If Nc7+ is played now, we can just go ...Kd8 and the knight is stuck.} 10...Ra5 11.a4 {White protects the knight by advancing their a-pawn. Now we advance our b-pawn one final time, attacking the bishop.} 11...b3 12.Bb1 {White retreats their bishop.} 12...e6 {And we play ...e6, bolstering d5 and opening up the f8-bishop. White's position is simply terrible. We'll proceed by developing our bishops and castling our king.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 1: White Plays The c3 and e3 Setup  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops the g1-knight. Roughly 50% of all Londons will end up looking like this. White allows us an easy plan now. We'll start by bringing our queen out and attacking the b2-pawn.} 5...Qb6 6.Qc2 {White can also defend the b-pawn without offering a queen trade, played 20% of the time. Against Qc2, we'll develop our bishop and attack the queen! Technically, dxc5 can be played, but few players find that move. The idea is that if White naively takes the bishop, we capture the b2-pawn and win White's rook.} 6...Bf5 7.Qc1 {White's other option after ...Bf5 is to slide the queen back a square. But this is admitting that we outplayed them. We'll capture the d4-pawn first.} 7...cxd4 8.exd4 {White recaptures with the e-pawn. Recapturing with the c-pawn would have immediately failed to ...Nb4. Now we bring our rook to the c-file. The idea is to quickly exploit the placement of the White queen.} 8...Rc8 9.Nbd2 {White usually proceeds by developing their other knight.} 9...Nb4 {But now we jump our knight into the b4-square, exploiting the pin. Black has a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 1: White Plays The c3 and e3 Setup  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops the g1-knight. Roughly 50% of all Londons will end up looking like this. White allows us an easy plan now. We'll start by bringing our queen out and attacking the b2-pawn.} 5...Qb6 6.Qb3 {Most often, White brings their queen out and offers a trade. This happens 45% of the time. We'll respond by advancing our c-pawn and attacking the queen. This pawn push also closes in White's light-squared bishop.} 6...c4 7.Qxb6 {60% of the time, White trades. We recapture with our b-pawn. Our pawns have been doubled, but our rook is well placed on the a-file and we can quickly start advancing our b-pawn up the board.} 7...axb6 8.Nbd2 {White develops their other knight. We go ...b5, adding more support to the c4-pawn and taking space.} 8...b5 9.Be2 {White develops their light-squared bishop and gets ready to castle.} 9...Bf5 {And we develop our light-squared bishop to the most active square. Our position is fantastic and we can proceed with ...e6 and ...h6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 1: White Plays The c3 and e3 Setup  / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nd2 {White's second option is the master's approach, played 30% of the time. Against this, we'll go for ...g6. We're trying to play ...Nh5 and fianchetto our bishop, but also generally making a waiting move. The most natural moves, Bd3 and Ngf3, now run into ...Qb6, and once White goes for the common Qb3-Qc2 maneuver, we've got ...Bf5!} 5...g6 6.Ngf3 {White develops their other knight. This happens half the time. We follow up by bringing our queen to b6 and attacking the b2-pawn.} 6...Qb6 7.Qb3 {White responds by bringing their queen out and offering a trade. We advance our c-pawn, attacking White's queen and closing in White's light-squared bishop. We would rather White take.} 7...c4 8.Qc2 {White slides their queen back. Now we develop our light-squared bishop and attack White's queen. Developing with tempo is important.} 8...Bf5 9.Qc1 {White retreats the queen again. And now, instead of the ...Qd8 and ...b5 plan, we should go for ...Nh5. We want to grab White's bishop.} 9...Nh5 10.Bg5 {White tries to keep their bishop. We attack the bishop with ...h6.} 10...h6 11.Bh4 {White slides their bishop back. Now we attack it again with ...g5.} 11...g5 12.Bg3 {White moves the bishop to safety. We finally capture the bishop. White's setup allowed us to expand and activate with a lot of tempi moves and eventually remove one of their active pieces.} 12...Nxg3 13.hxg3 {White recaptures with the h-pawn. There is now a threat of Nxg5.} 13...Bg7 {But we can simply play ...Bg7. We've got the bishop pair, more activity and a space advantage. We'll castle kingside and should trade if White ever plays b3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 1: White Plays The c3 and e3 Setup  / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops the g1-knight. Roughly 50% of all Londons will end up looking like this. White allows us an easy plan now. We'll start by bringing our queen out and attacking the b2-pawn.} 5...Qb6 6.b3 {White's third option is to defend against ...Qxb2 by advancing b3. This is played 15% of the time. Against this, we take the d4-pawn.} 6...cxd4 7.exd4 {White recaptures with the e-pawn. Now we develop our light-squared bishop to the most active square.} 7...Bf5 8.Bd3 {White develops their light-squared bishop, offering a trade of bishops. We'll respond by trading bishops.} 8...Bxd3 9.Qxd3 {White recaptures with the queen. Now we play ...e6, supporting the d5-pawn and opening our bishop.} 9...e6 10.O-O {White moves their king to safety.} 10...Be7 {And we simply develop our bishop. The position is in the balance, but the engine gives us a slight edge. We'll castle and bring our rook to the c-file. There are also some ideas to advance the a-pawn.} *

