1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops the g1-knight. Roughly 50% of all Londons will end up looking like this. White allows us an easy plan now. We'll start by bringing our queen out and attacking the b2-pawn.} 5...Qb6 6.b3 {White's third option is to defend against ...Qxb2 by advancing b3. This is played 15% of the time. Against this, we take the d4-pawn.} 6...cxd4 7.cxd4 {White recaptures with the c-pawn. We still develop our light-squared bishop to the most active square.} 7...Bf5 8.Bd3 {And when White offers a trade...} 8...Qa5+ {We've got the powerful ...Qa5+! White must now go Kf1 as any other move leads to a loss of material. Black is much better here.} *

