[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Bd3 {White's third option is to develop their light-squared bishop to d3. This is played 22% of the time. Even against Bd3, we'll bring our queen out and attack the b2-pawn. White can play Qc2 now, but our plan then is simply ...c4 and ...Bf5.} 5...Qb6 6.b3 {White's second option is to defend against ...Qxb2 by advancing b3. This happens 22% of the time.} 6...Bg4 {Against this, we'll develop our bishop and attack White's queen. Our position is quite comfortable. From here, we have got the simple plan ...e6, ...Be7 and castling, but throwing in ...Nh5 is also possible. If f3 is played, we can simply go ...Bh5 and bring our bishop to g6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 1: White Plays The c3 and e3 Setup  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops the g1-knight. Roughly 50% of all Londons will end up looking like this. White allows us an easy plan now. We'll start by bringing our queen out and attacking the b2-pawn.} 5...Qb6 6.Qb3 {Most often, White brings their queen out and offers a trade. This happens 45% of the time. We'll respond by advancing our c-pawn and attacking the queen. This pawn push also closes in White's light-squared bishop.} 6...c4 7.Qc2 {White's other option is to retreat. Against this, we'll go ...Bf5 and attack White's queen once more! At first glance, it might seem that the bishop can be captured, but Qxf5 immediately loses to ...Qxb2.} 7...Bf5 8.Qc1 {White moves the queen back one square, still guarding the b2-pawn. Now we play ...e6, bolstering d5 and opening up the f8-bishop.} 8...e6 9.Nbd2 {White develops their other knight. Now an important move: we slide the queen back to its home square. The point is to free up the b-pawn. We're anticipating that White might try to undermine our structure.} 9...Qd8 10.b3 {White goes b3, trying to undermine our pawn wedge on the queenside. We will advance our b-pawn in order to support the c4-pawn. We are always one step ahead!} 10...b5 11.a4 {And when White tries to undermine further by pushing their a-pawn...} 11...a6 {We are just in time with ...a6. Our position is comfortable and we can proceed with ...Be7 and ...O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 1: White Plays The c3 and e3 Setup  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops the g1-knight. Roughly 50% of all Londons will end up looking like this. White allows us an easy plan now. We'll start by bringing our queen out and attacking the b2-pawn.} 5...Qb6 6.Qc2 {White can also defend the b-pawn without offering a queen trade, played 20% of the time. Against Qc2, we'll develop our bishop and attack the queen! Technically, dxc5 can be played, but few players find that move. The idea is that if White naively takes the bishop, we capture the b2-pawn and win White's rook.} 6...Bf5 7.Bd3 {White develops their light-squared bishop and interposes the attack. This is the most common response to ...Bf5, but a pretty big blunder! We'll capture White's bishop.} 7...Bxd3 8.Qxd3 {White recaptures with their queen. And we take the b2-pawn! We're going to win White's rook, but our queen is almost trapped and we need to be very careful.} 8...Qxb2 9.O-O {White usually castles their king. Now we capture the free rook.} 9...Qxa1 10.Qc2 {White brings their queen back to defend the a2-pawn and prevent our queen from escaping via b2. The imminent threat is Nbd2, after which we would be forced to give up our queen for the other rook. We capture the d4-pawn. Now a move like Nbd2 doesn't work since we've got ...Qxc3.} 10...cxd4 11.cxd4 {White recaptures with the c-pawn. We jump our knight into the center. The point is that Nbd2 now doesn't trap our queen since there's ...Qc3.} 11...Ne4 12.Nfd2 {White brings their knight back with the idea to either trap our queen or trade off our centralized knight. But the knight has now taken its eyes off the d4-pawn... So we sacrifice our knight on d4 in order to finally liberate our queen!} 12...Nxd4 13.exd4 {White captures the knight. We take the pawn with our queen.} 13...Qxd4 14.Nxe4 {White usually takes the e4-knight.} 14...Qxe4 {And we recapture with our queen. Black is completely winning. We're up an exchange and three pawns!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 1: White Plays The c3 and e3 Setup  / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/3f88b34d-8f9f-4d99-be74-14d8a8ef394c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to d4 Sidelines for Black! This course will teach you how to fight against various 1. d4 sidelines. In this chapter, we'll be taking a look at how to fight the London.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to the f4-square, which characterizes the London. There are nearly 40 million games in the database from this position. It can be reached from multiple move orders, but that shouldn't affect our plan. Against the London, we should take the center with our d-pawn.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. And now we advance our c-pawn, putting pressure on White's center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White responds with c3, further supporting their central pawn. This is the standard London approach for both sides. White getting a dark-squared pawn chain and Black trying to challenge the center. We'll develop behind our pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Bd3 {White's third option is to develop their light-squared bishop to d3. This is played 22% of the time. Even against Bd3, we'll bring our queen out and attack the b2-pawn. White can play Qc2 now, but our plan then is simply ...c4 and ...Bf5.} 5...Qb6 6.Qc1 {White can also defend the b-pawn without offering a trade of queens. This happens 20% of the time. Against this, we'll take the d-pawn.} 6...cxd4 7.exd4 {And when White recaptures... We can strike in the center!} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {White takes with the d-pawn. And we respond with ...Ng4. The pawns on f2 and e5 are now under attack. This ...e5 and ...Ng4 idea exploits the weakness of Qc1: White now doesn't have Qe2 to defend both the pawn on f2 and pawn on e5.} 8...Ng4 9.Nh3 {White defends f2 with their knight.} 9...Ngxe5 {And we simply grab the e5-pawn. Black is doing extremely well here. White's pieces are misplaced and ...Bxh3 will ruin White's kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 2: Other Setups With Nbd2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/40d680f9-58c8-4369-bf08-fb35d96af8de/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this first chapter, we'll cover London setups that don't involve e3 and c3 together. We will learn how to deal with the Nbd2 setup, and check out some of White's other options move 4.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White goes for the London by developing their bishop to f4. Now we take the center with ...d5.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. We strike at the center with ...c5. We know what to do if White now bolsters their center with c3, but now there are other options.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {White's first option is to simply develop their kingside knight. This happens 21% of the time. Now we develop our other knight.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White can also develop their light-squared bishop and pin our knight. This is played 10% of the time. They want to put pressure on our weak piece and possibly play Ne5 right away. We respond ...Qa5+ with a double attack on White's king and bishop.} 5...Qa5+ 6.Nc3 {White has to go Nc3, blocking the check and defending the bishop. Now we jump our knight forward and put pressure on the c3-knight. We're doing to White what they wanted to do to us.} 6...Ne4 7.Bxc6+ {White usually takes our knight. We recapture with the c-pawn. The knight on c3 is still vulnerable.} 7...bxc6 8.O-O {White moves their king to safety. And we capture the knight. If White now tries to be tricky by pinning our knight with Qd2, we have ...Ne2+, followed by ...Ba6.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures. Now I suggest we go ...g6 and prepare to fianchetto our bishop. Taking the pawn was possible, but White gets an annoying initiative after a move like Ne5.} 9...g6 10.Ne5 {White usually jumps their knight forward and attacks the c6-pawn anyway. This, however, is a mistake! We can simply attack the knight.} 10...f6 11.Nxc6 {And if White takes the pawn...} 11...Qb5 {We have ...Qb5 to trap the knight. White now likely has to play Nb8 and get a rook and pawn for two pieces. Black is much better here. If our queen was on c3, this wouldn't have been possible!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 2: Other Setups With Nbd2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/40d680f9-58c8-4369-bf08-fb35d96af8de/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this first chapter, we'll cover London setups that don't involve e3 and c3 together. We will learn how to deal with the Nbd2 setup, and check out some of White's other options move 4.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White goes for the London by developing their bishop to f4. Now we take the center with ...d5.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. We strike at the center with ...c5. We know what to do if White now bolsters their center with c3, but now there are other options.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {White's first option is to simply develop their kingside knight. This happens 21% of the time. Now we develop our other knight.} 4...Nc6 5.Nbd2 {White develops their other knight. This isn't very popular, but it's considered to be the best setup. ...Qb6 would now lead to crazy complications, so we'll avoid it. I suggest we capture the d4-pawn.} 5...cxd4 6.exd4 {White recaptures with the e-pawn. And we develop our light-squared bishop to the most active square. We want to go ...e6, develop our other bishop and castle kingside.} 6...Bf5 7.c3 {Roughly 70% of the time, White goes c3, bolstering their center. Now we play ...e6, preparing to develop our dark-squared bishop.} 7...e6 8.Bb5 {Most often, White develops their other bishop and pins our knight. Their plan is to go ...Ne5 and put some pressure on the c6-knight. We'll respond by developing the f8-bishop and offering a trade.} 8...Bd6 9.Ne5 {Expect White to shove their knight into e5 at this point. We will just add a defender to the knight. Nothing to worry about, as White's knight is pinned to the bishop now as well.} 9...Qc7 10.Qa4 {And if White tries to put even more pressure with a move like Qa4...} 10...O-O {We can simply castle our king! The point is that White can't go Nxc6 because of ...Bxf4, and if Bxc6 instead, we play ...bxc6. Black is doing very well here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 2: Other Setups With Nbd2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/40d680f9-58c8-4369-bf08-fb35d96af8de/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this first chapter, we'll cover London setups that don't involve e3 and c3 together. We will learn how to deal with the Nbd2 setup, and check out some of White's other options move 4.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White goes for the London by developing their bishop to f4. Now we take the center with ...d5.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. We strike at the center with ...c5. We know what to do if White now bolsters their center with c3, but now there are other options.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {White's first option is to simply develop their kingside knight. This happens 21% of the time. Now we develop our other knight.} 4...Nc6 5.Nbd2 {White develops their other knight. This isn't very popular, but it's considered to be the best setup. ...Qb6 would now lead to crazy complications, so we'll avoid it. I suggest we capture the d4-pawn.} 5...cxd4 6.exd4 {White recaptures with the e-pawn. And we develop our light-squared bishop to the most active square. We want to go ...e6, develop our other bishop and castle kingside.} 6...Bf5 7.c3 {Roughly 70% of the time, White goes c3, bolstering their center. Now we play ...e6, preparing to develop our dark-squared bishop.} 7...e6 8.Be2 {White's other option is to develop their light-squared bishop to e2.} 8...Bd6 {Against this, we'll also respond ...Bd6 and offer a bishop trade. Black is comfortable here and we have a handful of plans: castling, ...Qc7, ...Ne4 and even ...Rab8 to start advancing on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 2: Other Setups With Nbd2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/40d680f9-58c8-4369-bf08-fb35d96af8de/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this first chapter, we'll cover London setups that don't involve e3 and c3 together. We will learn how to deal with the Nbd2 setup, and check out some of White's other options move 4.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White goes for the London by developing their bishop to f4. Now we take the center with ...d5.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. We strike at the center with ...c5. We know what to do if White now bolsters their center with c3, but now there are other options.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {White's first option is to simply develop their kingside knight. This happens 21% of the time. Now we develop our other knight.} 4...Nc6 5.Nbd2 {White develops their other knight. This isn't very popular, but it's considered to be the best setup. ...Qb6 would now lead to crazy complications, so we'll avoid it. I suggest we capture the d4-pawn.} 5...cxd4 6.exd4 {White recaptures with the e-pawn. And we develop our light-squared bishop to the most active square. We want to go ...e6, develop our other bishop and castle kingside.} 6...Bf5 7.Bb5 {White can also develop their light-squared bishop and pin our knight. Against this, we should play ...e6 to open up our dark-squared bishop.} 7...e6 8.Ne5 {White jumps their knight forward and puts pressure on our knight.} 8...Qb6 {And we go ...Qb6, supporting our knight and attacking the bishop. Black is completely fine here. If a4 we go ...a6, and if c4 we should just capture the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 2: Other Setups With Nbd2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/40d680f9-58c8-4369-bf08-fb35d96af8de/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this first chapter, we'll cover London setups that don't involve e3 and c3 together. We will learn how to deal with the Nbd2 setup, and check out some of White's other options move 4.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White goes for the London by developing their bishop to f4. Now we take the center with ...d5.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. We strike at the center with ...c5. We know what to do if White now bolsters their center with c3, but now there are other options.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {White's first option is to simply develop their kingside knight. This happens 21% of the time. Now we develop our other knight.} 4...Nc6 5.Nbd2 {White develops their other knight. This isn't very popular, but it's considered to be the best setup. ...Qb6 would now lead to crazy complications, so we'll avoid it. I suggest we capture the d4-pawn.} 5...cxd4 6.exd4 {White recaptures with the e-pawn. And we develop our light-squared bishop to the most active square. We want to go ...e6, develop our other bishop and castle kingside.} 6...Bf5 7.Bb5 {White can also develop their light-squared bishop and pin our knight. Against this, we should play ...e6 to open up our dark-squared bishop.} 7...e6 8.O-O {White can also simply castle.} 8...Be7 {We develop our dark-squared bishop and prepare castling. The position is in the balance. If Ne5 we can play ...Qb6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 2: Other Setups With Nbd2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/40d680f9-58c8-4369-bf08-fb35d96af8de/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this first chapter, we'll cover London setups that don't involve e3 and c3 together. We will learn how to deal with the Nbd2 setup, and check out some of White's other options move 4.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White goes for the London by developing their bishop to f4. Now we take the center with ...d5.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. We strike at the center with ...c5. We know what to do if White now bolsters their center with c3, but now there are other options.} 3...c5 4.c4 {White advances their c-pawn only 3% of the time. They want as much tension in the center as possible. We respond by taking the d-pawn.} 4...cxd4 5.exd4 {White recaptures with the e-pawn. And now we turn this into a better version of the Panov Caro-Kann with ...g6, preparing to fianchetto.} 5...g6 6.Nc3 {White develops the b1-knight. We fianchetto our dark-squared bishop and get ready to castle. This is the standard approach against the Panov.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. We simply castle our king. Our d5-pawn is still protected and we have easy development ahead.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {White develops their light-squared bishop to e2 and prepares castling. Now that White has moved the bishop, we capture the c4-pawn.} 8...dxc4 9.Bxc4 {White recaptures with their bishop.} 9...Bg4 {And we develop our light-squared bishop to g4, pinning the f3-knight. Black is doing well here. Our immediate threat is ...Bxf3 to either ruin White's kingside or win the d4-pawn if White plays Qxf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The London System / Study 2: Other Setups With Nbd2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/499c7c16-852c-45c2-934c-99f0d4deb159/studies/40d680f9-58c8-4369-bf08-fb35d96af8de/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this first chapter, we'll cover London setups that don't involve e3 and c3 together. We will learn how to deal with the Nbd2 setup, and check out some of White's other options move 4.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {White goes for the London by developing their bishop to f4. Now we take the center with ...d5.} 2...d5 3.e3 {White bolsters their center and opens up the light-squared bishop. We strike at the center with ...c5. We know what to do if White now bolsters their center with c3, but now there are other options.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White can also take the c-pawn. dxc5 only happens roughly 4% of the time, but it is important for us to know how to fight this capture. We'll respond ...e6, opening up the f8-bishop and threatening to take.} 4...e6 5.b4 {White advances their b-pawn in order to support to the c5-pawn. They will probably do this if they choose to capture on c5. That's why we have to be prepared to undermine the structure immediately.} 5...a5 6.c3 {White supports their b-pawn. We capture the b4-pawn.} 6...axb4 7.cxb4 {White recaptures with the c-pawn.} 7...Bd7 {And we play ...Bd7, covering b5 and stopping any Bb5 ideas. The position is balanced. We'll go ...b6 and White won't be able to hold onto their extra pawn. It's all about the pawn structure here.} *

