[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 1: White plays 3.Bxf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/b3b919cf-8cb3-4c93-a10f-f4720c0d8ef2/lines"]

1.d4 {As we already know, the dark-squared bishop can become a problem for White in 1.d4 positions if they have to play e3 before developing it. One solution for that is the London, which we've already seen. In Chapter 3, we'll take a look at another one, which is the Trompowsky, characterized by White developing their bishop to g5 on move 2.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {And here comes the Trompowsky, played 4% of the time and in almost 10 million games. Most often, the idea is to simply remove our knight from f6 and never worry about the bishop again, which we'll cover in Study 1. Going for ...d5 works almost every time. We simply contest the center and await White's next move.} 2...d5 3.Bxf6 {White captures on f6 more than 40% of the time, which seems to be the whole point of the opening. They want to complement the absence of their bishop with pawns on dark squares and force us to play positions without our important kingside knight. I recommend recapturing with the g-pawn! The idea is simple: ...e5 is coming.} 3...gxf6 4.e3 {White traded off their bishop and begins placing pawns on dark squares. But can't stop our central expansion.} 4...e5 5.c4 {White can opt for the active 5.c4 with more tension in the center, which you will face almost as often as 5.c3. It might seem tempting to play ...c6 and provide reinforcement, but we need the c6-square for the knight. Our d5-pawn is defended anyway and it's more important to develop and put pressure on the center ourselves.} 5...Nc6 6.cxd5 {It's logical for White to capture on d5. And bring our queen there.} 6...Qxd5 7.Nc3 {So that they can attack it. However, chess is not that easy. We don't have to retreat here and give White the upper hand and d5 opportunities. We can pin the knight instead and keep our queen in the center.} 7...Bb4 8.a3 {If White attacks it, we have no choice. We have to remove the knight.} 8...Bxc3+ 9.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures. These trades are good for us because we have an active queen and we provoked a weakness from White on the c3-square. Which we can now attack with ...Qa5.} 9...Qa5 10.Qd2 {White defends.} 10...Be6 {And we can bring the bishop to the natural e6-square. All of our pieces are active and we have a great position. We'll castle long and take advantage of the semi-open g-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 1: White plays 3.Bxf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/b3b919cf-8cb3-4c93-a10f-f4720c0d8ef2/lines"]

1.d4 {As we already know, the dark-squared bishop can become a problem for White in 1.d4 positions if they have to play e3 before developing it. One solution for that is the London, which we've already seen. In Chapter 3, we'll take a look at another one, which is the Trompowsky, characterized by White developing their bishop to g5 on move 2.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {And here comes the Trompowsky, played 4% of the time and in almost 10 million games. Most often, the idea is to simply remove our knight from f6 and never worry about the bishop again, which we'll cover in Study 1. Going for ...d5 works almost every time. We simply contest the center and await White's next move.} 2...d5 3.Bxf6 {White captures on f6 more than 40% of the time, which seems to be the whole point of the opening. They want to complement the absence of their bishop with pawns on dark squares and force us to play positions without our important kingside knight. I recommend recapturing with the g-pawn! The idea is simple: ...e5 is coming.} 3...gxf6 4.e3 {White traded off their bishop and begins placing pawns on dark squares. But can't stop our central expansion.} 4...e5 5.c3 {White supports the center just over 20% of the time. It's definitely a solid but more passive route than 5.c4. Let's try to exploit it by responding actively and create total chaos in the center. White's structure is under attack from both the e5- and the c5-pawns.} 5...c5 6.Nf3 {Our main line will be 6.Nf3, which is played 25% of the time, as mentioned. There's more pressure in the center, but Black already has an advantage. We'll defend with the knight.} 6...Nc6 7.Bb5 {Similar to lines we've covered before, we have a good answer if White pins our knight. And that's to bring the rook to g8 and attack the g2-pawn.} 7...Rg8 8.O-O {White castling could become dangerous. First, we'll slide the queen to b6 and attack White's bishop.} 8...Qb6 9.Bxc6+ {This basically forces a trade. We'll recapture with the queen and keep our pawn structure intact. But did we hang a pawn?} 9...Qxc6 10.dxe5 {White capturing in the center is no good.} 10...Bh3 {After ...Bh3, they are already close to being lost. The g2-pawn is too vulnerable. If White plays g3, capturing the rook on f1 is playable, but I advise against it. Simply ...O-O-O keeps the attack going with a huge advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 1: White plays 3.Bxf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/b3b919cf-8cb3-4c93-a10f-f4720c0d8ef2/lines"]

1.d4 {As we already know, the dark-squared bishop can become a problem for White in 1.d4 positions if they have to play e3 before developing it. One solution for that is the London, which we've already seen. In Chapter 3, we'll take a look at another one, which is the Trompowsky, characterized by White developing their bishop to g5 on move 2.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {And here comes the Trompowsky, played 4% of the time and in almost 10 million games. Most often, the idea is to simply remove our knight from f6 and never worry about the bishop again, which we'll cover in Study 1. Going for ...d5 works almost every time. We simply contest the center and await White's next move.} 2...d5 3.Bxf6 {White captures on f6 more than 40% of the time, which seems to be the whole point of the opening. They want to complement the absence of their bishop with pawns on dark squares and force us to play positions without our important kingside knight. I recommend recapturing with the g-pawn! The idea is simple: ...e5 is coming.} 3...gxf6 4.e3 {White traded off their bishop and begins placing pawns on dark squares. But can't stop our central expansion.} 4...e5 5.c3 {White supports the center just over 20% of the time. It's definitely a solid but more passive route than 5.c4. Let's try to exploit it by responding actively and create total chaos in the center. White's structure is under attack from both the e5- and the c5-pawns.} 5...c5 6.dxc5 {Capturing on c5 gives up the center completely. And activates our dark-squared bishop.} 6...Bxc5 7.Nd2 {White continues with Nd2, aiming for the b3-square. We don't have to worry about that yet. Let's simply continue development.} 7...Nc6 8.Nb3 {White rotates to b3 and attacks our bishop, but we can simply bring it back to b6. White spent two moves to improve a piece and it doesn't have much potential anyway. We have a big advantage and it shows.} 8...Bb6 9.Bb5 {Don't be afraid of this pin. Simply strengthening the center works.} 9...Be6 10.Nf3 {White will develop the knight at some point.} 10...Rg8 {But we can simply continue playing natural moves as we have throughout this line and we are much better. Moves like ...a6, ...Qd7 and long castling come to mind. Capturing on c5 rarely pays off for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 1: White plays 3.Bxf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/b3b919cf-8cb3-4c93-a10f-f4720c0d8ef2/lines"]

1.d4 {As we already know, the dark-squared bishop can become a problem for White in 1.d4 positions if they have to play e3 before developing it. One solution for that is the London, which we've already seen. In Chapter 3, we'll take a look at another one, which is the Trompowsky, characterized by White developing their bishop to g5 on move 2.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {And here comes the Trompowsky, played 4% of the time and in almost 10 million games. Most often, the idea is to simply remove our knight from f6 and never worry about the bishop again, which we'll cover in Study 1. Going for ...d5 works almost every time. We simply contest the center and await White's next move.} 2...d5 3.Bxf6 {White captures on f6 more than 40% of the time, which seems to be the whole point of the opening. They want to complement the absence of their bishop with pawns on dark squares and force us to play positions without our important kingside knight. I recommend recapturing with the g-pawn! The idea is simple: ...e5 is coming.} 3...gxf6 4.e3 {White traded off their bishop and begins placing pawns on dark squares. But can't stop our central expansion.} 4...e5 5.Nf3 {White brings the knight out 15% of the time. It seems unorthodox to allow ...e4 like this and make the knight a target, but that is exactly what White wants. Pushing ...e4 would commit our pawns to d5 and e4 and leave our f-pawns doubled and meaningless. Let's defend the center instead, since it was under attack.} 5...Nc6 6.Bb5 {White pins our knight and reintroduces the threat of capturing in the center. But we've got a strong response against this. The bishop left its post on f1, so we can make use of the g-file and attack the g2-pawn! White has to make a decision.} 6...Rg8 7.O-O {Castling is a mistake.} 7...Bg4 {White is already in trouble after ...Bg4. They won't be able to capture in the center now and ...Qd7 is coming, with what's potentially a strong attack. We can castle long and develop the dark-squared bishop to d6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 1: White plays 3.Bxf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/b3b919cf-8cb3-4c93-a10f-f4720c0d8ef2/lines"]

1.d4 {As we already know, the dark-squared bishop can become a problem for White in 1.d4 positions if they have to play e3 before developing it. One solution for that is the London, which we've already seen. In Chapter 3, we'll take a look at another one, which is the Trompowsky, characterized by White developing their bishop to g5 on move 2.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {And here comes the Trompowsky, played 4% of the time and in almost 10 million games. Most often, the idea is to simply remove our knight from f6 and never worry about the bishop again, which we'll cover in Study 1. Going for ...d5 works almost every time. We simply contest the center and await White's next move.} 2...d5 3.Bxf6 {White captures on f6 more than 40% of the time, which seems to be the whole point of the opening. They want to complement the absence of their bishop with pawns on dark squares and force us to play positions without our important kingside knight. I recommend recapturing with the g-pawn! The idea is simple: ...e5 is coming.} 3...gxf6 4.e3 {White traded off their bishop and begins placing pawns on dark squares. But can't stop our central expansion.} 4...e5 5.c4 {White can opt for the active 5.c4 with more tension in the center, which you will face almost as often as 5.c3. It might seem tempting to play ...c6 and provide reinforcement, but we need the c6-square for the knight. Our d5-pawn is defended anyway and it's more important to develop and put pressure on the center ourselves.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {If White brings the knight to c3, we can begin capturing. On d4 first.} 6...exd4 7.exd4 {White recaptures with the pawn.} 7...dxc4 {And now on c4 as well. We made these trades to isolate White's d4-pawn and make it weak. It's already targeted by two pieces. If White pushes d5, we have ...Qe7+ followed by ...Ne5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 2: White plays 3.e3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/502e8622-178f-41b4-86b3-34558a8c3b5d/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will focus on White choosing to play e3 on move 3 rather than Bxf6. In an unconventional Trompowsky approach, White claims their bishop on g5 is better and more active than our knight on f6 and wants to keep it there. Let's begin.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {The Trompowsky. Let's expand in the center.} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is played 25% of the time, in almost a million games. White connects the pawns and opens up the light-squared bishop. One thing to keep in mind for us is that when White keeps the bishop on g5, ...Ne4 is a good response to attack it at a certain point. But we'll go ...c5 for now. Don't go ...e6 and pin your own knight while blocking your bishop.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White plays c3 almost half the time here, as they want to reinforce the center. However, there is something else we can take advantage of, and that's the b2-pawn being vulnerable. When White moves the dark-squared bishop away early and we play ...c5, this is a common idea for Black.} 4...Qb6 5.Qb3 {White offers a trade of queens. We don't want to capture and open up the a-file for them. In fact, we would much prefer them to capture on b6. Which is why ...c4 is a great move to ask the queen a question.} 5...c4 6.Qc2 {If White doesn't want to help us activate our pieces, they will retreat to c2 and continue defending the b2-pawn. We'll get a bunch of activity and tempi in this line either way. We'll jump to e4 in that case and attack the bishop.} 6...Ne4 7.Bf4 {There is another benefit to ...Ne4. And that's to develop to f5 and threaten ...Ng3 with a discovered attack on the queen.} 7...Bf5 8.Qc1 {White has to resort to even more passivity and retreat to c1. But we're not done applying pressure. Let's go after their bishop now!} 8...g5 9.Be5 {White can move to the center before returning back to g3. This provokes ...f6, but we're okay with that.} 9...f6 10.Bg3 {Now the bishop has to retreat to g3. We didn't force it there to capture it, however. We only wanted to expand and we don't want to open up the h-file for them now.} 10...Nc6 {Let's develop instead and look to trap the bishop with ...h5 and ...h4. Our king is safe in the center for now and we keep our options open for castling long if needed. Black is on the verge of being winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 2: White plays 3.e3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/502e8622-178f-41b4-86b3-34558a8c3b5d/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will focus on White choosing to play e3 on move 3 rather than Bxf6. In an unconventional Trompowsky approach, White claims their bishop on g5 is better and more active than our knight on f6 and wants to keep it there. Let's begin.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {The Trompowsky. Let's expand in the center.} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is played 25% of the time, in almost a million games. White connects the pawns and opens up the light-squared bishop. One thing to keep in mind for us is that when White keeps the bishop on g5, ...Ne4 is a good response to attack it at a certain point. But we'll go ...c5 for now. Don't go ...e6 and pin your own knight while blocking your bishop.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White plays c3 almost half the time here, as they want to reinforce the center. However, there is something else we can take advantage of, and that's the b2-pawn being vulnerable. When White moves the dark-squared bishop away early and we play ...c5, this is a common idea for Black.} 4...Qb6 5.Qb3 {White offers a trade of queens. We don't want to capture and open up the a-file for them. In fact, we would much prefer them to capture on b6. Which is why ...c4 is a great move to ask the queen a question.} 5...c4 6.Qxb6 {White takes on b6, which plays into our hands. We'll recapture and now our rook is open, and we have prospects of pushing the b-pawn down the line.} 6...axb6 7.Nd2 {For now, let's finish development. White activates their knight to d2.} 7...Nbd7 {And we will support ours with ...Nbd7. Moves like ...e6 and ...b5 are coming, with a healthy pawn chain and potential for expansion. Our dark-squared bishop will be open and we don't have to worry about our c8-bishop yet. Its time will come.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 2: White plays 3.e3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/502e8622-178f-41b4-86b3-34558a8c3b5d/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will focus on White choosing to play e3 on move 3 rather than Bxf6. In an unconventional Trompowsky approach, White claims their bishop on g5 is better and more active than our knight on f6 and wants to keep it there. Let's begin.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {The Trompowsky. Let's expand in the center.} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is played 25% of the time, in almost a million games. White connects the pawns and opens up the light-squared bishop. One thing to keep in mind for us is that when White keeps the bishop on g5, ...Ne4 is a good response to attack it at a certain point. But we'll go ...c5 for now. Don't go ...e6 and pin your own knight while blocking your bishop.} 3...c5 4.Bxf6 {White captures on f6 now 10% of the time. We'll capture with the g-pawn as we did in Study 1. However, the difference here is that we haven't played ...e5 yet so White can capture on c5.} 4...gxf6 5.dxc5 {Which they do. Going for ...e5 now could be too dangerous as White would have the strong Qf3. So I suggest the more solid ...e6, protecting the center and attacking the c5-pawn.} 5...e6 6.c4 {Don't be afraid of c4. White does undermine our center, but we can always choose to simplify. It's better to capture on c4 and keep our pawns together than allow White to capture and isolate them.} 6...dxc4 7.Qxd8+ {We don't even mind a queen trade. It means less chances for White to attack.} 7...Kxd8 8.Bxc4 {If they capture on c4...} 8...Bxc5 {We'll take on c5, with an easy position. Our ideas are ...Ke7 to improve the king and prepare for the endgame, ...Nc6 to develop the knight to the best square and ...Bd7. Our rooks will be connected and ready to join the game in the long run.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Trompowsky / Study 2: White plays 3.e3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/28f029cd-1acb-4885-b33b-96fff81ea97a/studies/502e8622-178f-41b4-86b3-34558a8c3b5d/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will focus on White choosing to play e3 on move 3 rather than Bxf6. In an unconventional Trompowsky approach, White claims their bishop on g5 is better and more active than our knight on f6 and wants to keep it there. Let's begin.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {The Trompowsky. Let's expand in the center.} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is played 25% of the time, in almost a million games. White connects the pawns and opens up the light-squared bishop. One thing to keep in mind for us is that when White keeps the bishop on g5, ...Ne4 is a good response to attack it at a certain point. But we'll go ...c5 for now. Don't go ...e6 and pin your own knight while blocking your bishop.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is flexible and calm, allowing us to move forward with our plans. It's played 11% of the time. We'll jump to the center and attack the bishop first.} 4...Ne4 5.Bh4 {Once it retreats to h4... We can capture in the center once.} 5...cxd4 6.exd4 {Our point was to force the bishop to h4 first and then relieve the tension in the center. So that we can attack some weaknesses unbothered.} 6...Qb6 {The queen pressures the b2-pawn, and White can't retreat the dark-squared bishop to defend. Our next ideas include ...Nc6 and the odd ...e5, a pawn sacrifice where we follow up with ...Bc5 and ...Bf5 or ...Bg4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Stonewall System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3, c3 and f4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/929b1b35-4701-47f4-902c-7ae7fa8c5235/studies/414835dc-60c0-44da-a63a-cd5f4d4bfeae/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's analyze how to face the Stonewall setup as Black now. The setup got its name for its very solid appearance which looks hard to crack. That means it's extremely important for Black to stay active.} 1...Nf6 2.e3 {White plays e3, going for the solid Stonewall setup with c3 and f4. The big downside of this approach is that their bishop on c1 will be stuck behind their pawn structure. Now it is time to push ...d5, opening up the light-squared bishop and controlling the central e4- and c4-squares.} 2...d5 3.Bd3 {White brings out the light-squared bishop, as they do in 20% of the games. Nf3 would block their f-pawn, so now we can be pretty sure that they'll go for the Stonewall with f4. We wait with the b8-knight's development and put some pressure on White's central d4-pawn.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White solidifies their center with c3, which is played 70% of the game. Now that we've pushed ...c5, it's time to develop the knight.} 4...Nc6 5.f4 {White has achieved their Stonewall structure. Looks difficult to crack, but on the other hand our minor pieces have much more freedom. Now we get to develop the bishop with tempo.} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {White blocks the attack by bringing the knight out. Our light-squared bishop is already developed so we can push ...e6 without any worry.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White gets castled. We bring out the bishop to d6 and look over the e5-square.} 7...Bd6 8.Nbd2 {White could develop here instead. We'll get the king to safety.} 8...O-O 9.h3 {White kicks our our bishop. ...Bf5 to trade off the bishop should be played here. This is a good general idea once your bishop is attacked on g4.} 9...Bf5 10.Bxf5 {White trades as retreating would only benefit us. We recapture and grip the e4-square.} 10...exf5 11.Ne5 {White centralizes their knight. But c8 is a great square for the rook, eyeing the c-file.} 11...Rc8 12.Qf3 {White begins to shift the queen to the kingside.} 12...Re8 {The same plan applies also here: We should centralize with ...Ne4, retreat the knight to e7 and kick away White's knight with ...f6. Against the Stonewall where White's key idea is to keep the knight on e5, we simply have to commit to ...f6 at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Stonewall System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3, c3 and f4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/929b1b35-4701-47f4-902c-7ae7fa8c5235/studies/414835dc-60c0-44da-a63a-cd5f4d4bfeae/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's analyze how to face the Stonewall setup as Black now. The setup got its name for its very solid appearance which looks hard to crack. That means it's extremely important for Black to stay active.} 1...Nf6 2.e3 {White plays e3, going for the solid Stonewall setup with c3 and f4. The big downside of this approach is that their bishop on c1 will be stuck behind their pawn structure. Now it is time to push ...d5, opening up the light-squared bishop and controlling the central e4- and c4-squares.} 2...d5 3.Bd3 {White brings out the light-squared bishop, as they do in 20% of the games. Nf3 would block their f-pawn, so now we can be pretty sure that they'll go for the Stonewall with f4. We wait with the b8-knight's development and put some pressure on White's central d4-pawn.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White solidifies their center with c3, which is played 70% of the game. Now that we've pushed ...c5, it's time to develop the knight.} 4...Nc6 5.f4 {White has achieved their Stonewall structure. Looks difficult to crack, but on the other hand our minor pieces have much more freedom. Now we get to develop the bishop with tempo.} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {White blocks the attack by bringing the knight out. Our light-squared bishop is already developed so we can push ...e6 without any worry.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White gets castled. We bring out the bishop to d6 and look over the e5-square.} 7...Bd6 8.Qe1 {White shifts their queen to e1, aiming to join the attack on the kingside. We can safely castle here. Our minor pieces will do a good job defending the king.} 8...O-O 9.Ne5 {White has a strong grip on the e5-square, so they occupy it with the knight, which is one of their ideas in the Stonewall. White's light-squared bishop is a strong attacker, so trading it off is a good idea here.} 9...Bf5 10.Bxf5 {White doesn't give us the diagonal by retreating and trades instead. We'll recapture with a strong grip over the e4-square. These are the important dynamics one must know in positions like this one. Instead of trading our bishop for White's knight, we trade it for their bishop!} 10...exf5 11.Nd2 {White finally starts to develop their queenside. With ...Rc8 we can have future ideas of playing on the c-file with trading on d4. This move is simply a must.} 11...Rc8 12.Rf3 {White tries to bring more attackers to the kingside. But we have a solid grip over the light-squares and by centralizing the knight to e4 we prepare to kick away White's knight from e5 by pushing ...f6. White doesn't have this luxury since they've already pushed f4.} 12...Ne4 13.Rh3 {White slides the rook to h3.} 13...Ne7 {By retreating the knight to e7 we don't allow White to trade it off, but instead, we'll kick away their knight with ...f6 on the next move. The knight could also guard our king in case White starts to attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Stonewall System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3, c3 and f4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/929b1b35-4701-47f4-902c-7ae7fa8c5235/studies/414835dc-60c0-44da-a63a-cd5f4d4bfeae/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's analyze how to face the Stonewall setup as Black now. The setup got its name for its very solid appearance which looks hard to crack. That means it's extremely important for Black to stay active.} 1...Nf6 2.e3 {White plays e3, going for the solid Stonewall setup with c3 and f4. The big downside of this approach is that their bishop on c1 will be stuck behind their pawn structure. Now it is time to push ...d5, opening up the light-squared bishop and controlling the central e4- and c4-squares.} 2...d5 3.Bd3 {White brings out the light-squared bishop, as they do in 20% of the games. Nf3 would block their f-pawn, so now we can be pretty sure that they'll go for the Stonewall with f4. We wait with the b8-knight's development and put some pressure on White's central d4-pawn.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White solidifies their center with c3, which is played 70% of the game. Now that we've pushed ...c5, it's time to develop the knight.} 4...Nc6 5.f4 {White has achieved their Stonewall structure. Looks difficult to crack, but on the other hand our minor pieces have much more freedom. Now we get to develop the bishop with tempo.} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {White blocks the attack by bringing the knight out. Our light-squared bishop is already developed so we can push ...e6 without any worry.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White gets castled. We bring out the bishop to d6 and look over the e5-square.} 7...Bd6 8.Qe1 {White shifts their queen to e1, aiming to join the attack on the kingside. We can safely castle here. Our minor pieces will do a good job defending the king.} 8...O-O 9.Qh4 {White immediately brings the queen to the kingside. It's important that we guard the h7-square by offering to trade the bishops.} 9...Bf5 10.Be2 {White tries to preserve their light-squared attacker. But ...h6 guards the g5-square and prevents any Ng5 ideas.} 10...h6 11.h3 {White could perhaps push g4 in the future.} 11...Ne4 {But we respond with the knight's centralization and a queen trade offer. After the queens are off, the attack will be non-existent and we'll enjoy great piece placement and play on the queenside regarding the c-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Stonewall System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3, c3 and f4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/929b1b35-4701-47f4-902c-7ae7fa8c5235/studies/414835dc-60c0-44da-a63a-cd5f4d4bfeae/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's analyze how to face the Stonewall setup as Black now. The setup got its name for its very solid appearance which looks hard to crack. That means it's extremely important for Black to stay active.} 1...Nf6 2.e3 {White plays e3, going for the solid Stonewall setup with c3 and f4. The big downside of this approach is that their bishop on c1 will be stuck behind their pawn structure. Now it is time to push ...d5, opening up the light-squared bishop and controlling the central e4- and c4-squares.} 2...d5 3.Bd3 {White brings out the light-squared bishop, as they do in 20% of the games. Nf3 would block their f-pawn, so now we can be pretty sure that they'll go for the Stonewall with f4. We wait with the b8-knight's development and put some pressure on White's central d4-pawn.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White solidifies their center with c3, which is played 70% of the game. Now that we've pushed ...c5, it's time to develop the knight.} 4...Nc6 5.f4 {White has achieved their Stonewall structure. Looks difficult to crack, but on the other hand our minor pieces have much more freedom. Now we get to develop the bishop with tempo.} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {White blocks the attack by bringing the knight out. Our light-squared bishop is already developed so we can push ...e6 without any worry.} 6...e6 7.Nbd2 {White waits with castling and starts to develop their queenside instead. We bring out the bishop to its usual square in the Stonewall.} 7...Bd6 8.h3 {White asks our bishop a question. We'll answer with ...Bf5, aiming to trade off White's strong attacker.} 8...Bf5 9.Qc2 {White creates a battery. By trading on d4 we open up the c-file for our rook to generate counterplay there. We are simply playing around White's structure and trying to take advantage of any weak points.} 9...cxd4 10.cxd4 {White couldn't recapture with the e-pawn since the f4-pawn would hang. Now we trade, this was our goal.} 10...Bxd3 11.Qxd3 {White recaptures. We'll get the king to safety.} 11...O-O 12.a3 {White prevents ...Nb4.} 12...Rc8 {But our idea prevails after ...Rc8. We'll play on the queenside with moves like ...Qb6 and ...Na5, while White's attack on the kingside isn't promising.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Stonewall System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3, c3 and f4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/929b1b35-4701-47f4-902c-7ae7fa8c5235/studies/414835dc-60c0-44da-a63a-cd5f4d4bfeae/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's analyze how to face the Stonewall setup as Black now. The setup got its name for its very solid appearance which looks hard to crack. That means it's extremely important for Black to stay active.} 1...Nf6 2.e3 {White plays e3, going for the solid Stonewall setup with c3 and f4. The big downside of this approach is that their bishop on c1 will be stuck behind their pawn structure. Now it is time to push ...d5, opening up the light-squared bishop and controlling the central e4- and c4-squares.} 2...d5 3.Bd3 {White brings out the light-squared bishop, as they do in 20% of the games. Nf3 would block their f-pawn, so now we can be pretty sure that they'll go for the Stonewall with f4. We wait with the b8-knight's development and put some pressure on White's central d4-pawn.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White could grab the pawn on c5. This obviously isn't the Stonewall anymore, but still we should know what to do. It's very rare, played 7% of the time. In the absence of the d4-pawn, we can freely grab the center with ...e5!} 4...e5 5.b4 {White tries to hold onto the c5-pawn. We try to chip away at White's queenside structure by pushing ...a5.} 5...a5 6.c3 {White solidifies the b4-pawn. We'll trade.} 6...axb4 7.cxb4 {White recaptures.} 7...b6 {And we'll try to chip away at the c5-pawn yet again and enjoy the semi-open a-file for our rook. We should eventually come out on top and shatter White's pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Colle System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3 and Nf3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/d263613f-e7ee-43ee-beef-a7177421d52a/studies/1bd81b7c-9112-478b-9a2e-14bcdf8a9856/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 5, White adapts the so called "Colle System". It's similar to the Stonewall, but White brings out the knight to f3 and slowly aims to push e4. Let's check out how we should approach this setup!} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White brings out their knight as well, keeping their setup secret for now. We'll also place a pawn in the center.} 2...d5 3.e3 {With e3, our opponent goes for the Colle system, aiming to quickly bring out the light-squared bishop to d3 and push for e4. After the London, it is the second most popular move with 21% occurrence. White doesn't have the luxury of developing their queenside bishop, but we do, and we'll pin the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.Bd3 {The second most popular move occurs in 16% of the games, as White develops the bishop to d3 and controls the e4-square. We'll put some pressure on the d4-pawn with ...c5 before bringing out the knight to c6.} 4...c5 5.c3 {White supports the d-pawn with c3. Now it's the right time to develop the knight to its natural square. This position is The London but with the exception that White's bishop is stuck on c1!} 5...Nc6 6.Nbd2 {White develops the knight as well. But ...e6 adds some support to the d5-pawn and opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White gets their king to safety. We'll develop the bishop to d6 as Black should do in these structures without White's dark-squared bishop developed.} 7...Bd6 8.h3 {White kicks out our bishop. We'll keep the pin by retreating to h5.} 8...Bh5 9.Re1 {In The Colle System, White slowly prepares to push e4. Let's just castle the king and get it out of the center.} 9...O-O 10.e4 {This is the most popular, yet a bad plan. Our pieces have a lot of activity. Let's see how we can punish this move. First we trade, isolating the d4-pawn.} 10...cxd4 11.cxd4 {White recaptures. We'll trade in the center yet again.} 11...dxe4 12.Nxe4 {White recaptures. We'll save the bishop pair for now and attack White's rook.} 12...Bb4 13.Bd2 {White blocks the attack with their bishop.} 13...Bxd2 {And suddenly after a trade on d2, White's center is collapsing due to the pin on the f3-knight. Pushing in the center doesn't always pay off!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Colle System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3 and Nf3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/d263613f-e7ee-43ee-beef-a7177421d52a/studies/1bd81b7c-9112-478b-9a2e-14bcdf8a9856/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 5, White adapts the so called "Colle System". It's similar to the Stonewall, but White brings out the knight to f3 and slowly aims to push e4. Let's check out how we should approach this setup!} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White brings out their knight as well, keeping their setup secret for now. We'll also place a pawn in the center.} 2...d5 3.e3 {With e3, our opponent goes for the Colle system, aiming to quickly bring out the light-squared bishop to d3 and push for e4. After the London, it is the second most popular move with 21% occurrence. White doesn't have the luxury of developing their queenside bishop, but we do, and we'll pin the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {35% of the time White breaks the pin with Be2 and prepares to castle. We support the d5-pawn and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.O-O {White hides their king. We'll bring the bishop to d6 to cover the e5-square and prepare to push ...e5 in the future.} 5...Bd6 6.h3 {White attacks our bishop. We'll retreat to h5 and keep its diagonal.} 6...Bh5 7.c4 {White puts pressure on our central pawn. We'll reinforce it with ...c6. Pretty logical so far.} 7...c6 8.Nc3 {White starts to develop the queenside pieces. We do as well.} 8...Nbd7 9.c5 {White sees the opportunity to gain space with tempo by pushing c5 and play on the queenside with b4 and a4 next. It's important that we retreat to c7 and keep guarding the e5-square, so that we'll be able to push ...e5 in the next few moves.} 9...Bc7 10.b4 {White continues to grab space on the queenside. Committing to this queenside approach and closing down their central role is risky for White, as it gives us all the central control in terms of pawn breaks. Before initiating any action in the center, we should hide the king.} 10...O-O 11.a4 {White continues to expand. ...Re8 further prepares ...e5. It becomes pretty clear why ...e5 is our main idea.} 11...Re8 12.b5 {Our opponent plays on the queenside with b5 and tries to attack our pawn chain...} 12...e5 {But a good answer to a flank attack is to create action in the center! Black has a comfortable position with all of their pieces playing. There might be some trades incoming in case White captures on e5 with the knight, but we don't mind them. If not, we can consider pushing ...e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Colle System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3 and Nf3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/d263613f-e7ee-43ee-beef-a7177421d52a/studies/1bd81b7c-9112-478b-9a2e-14bcdf8a9856/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 5, White adapts the so called "Colle System". It's similar to the Stonewall, but White brings out the knight to f3 and slowly aims to push e4. Let's check out how we should approach this setup!} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White brings out their knight as well, keeping their setup secret for now. We'll also place a pawn in the center.} 2...d5 3.e3 {With e3, our opponent goes for the Colle system, aiming to quickly bring out the light-squared bishop to d3 and push for e4. After the London, it is the second most popular move with 21% occurrence. White doesn't have the luxury of developing their queenside bishop, but we do, and we'll pin the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {In 11% of the games White immediately kicks out our bishop from g4. We should always retreat to h5.} 4...Bh5 5.g4 {White attacks further with g4 to break the pin. We simply retreat to g6.} 5...Bg6 6.Ne5 {White goes after our light-squared bishop, since it's a pretty good piece. This is the most active plan; we keep the knight on b8 in reserve and challenge White's e5-knight. ...Nbd7 would allow h4 as White and suddenly Black's position is very passive.} 6...Nfd7 7.Nxg6 {White gets rid of our light-squared bishop. We recapture with the h-pawn, of course, opening up the rook.} 7...hxg6 8.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We will play ...e6 to support the d5-pawn and open up our light-squared bishop.} 8...e6 9.c4 {White challenges the d5-pawn which plays in with their fianchettoed bishop on g2.} 9...c6 {By pushing ...c6 we support the d5-pawn and block the view of the bishop on g2. Now if White plays Nc3, we can actually grab on c4 and defend it with ...Nb6.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Colle System / Study 1: White Plays d4, e3 and Nf3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/d263613f-e7ee-43ee-beef-a7177421d52a/studies/1bd81b7c-9112-478b-9a2e-14bcdf8a9856/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 5, White adapts the so called "Colle System". It's similar to the Stonewall, but White brings out the knight to f3 and slowly aims to push e4. Let's check out how we should approach this setup!} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White brings out their knight as well, keeping their setup secret for now. We'll also place a pawn in the center.} 2...d5 3.e3 {With e3, our opponent goes for the Colle system, aiming to quickly bring out the light-squared bishop to d3 and push for e4. After the London, it is the second most popular move with 21% occurrence. White doesn't have the luxury of developing their queenside bishop, but we do, and we'll pin the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {In 11% of the games White immediately kicks out our bishop from g4. We should always retreat to h5.} 4...Bh5 5.g4 {White attacks further with g4 to break the pin. We simply retreat to g6.} 5...Bg6 6.Ne5 {White goes after our light-squared bishop, since it's a pretty good piece. This is the most active plan; we keep the knight on b8 in reserve and challenge White's e5-knight. ...Nbd7 would allow h4 as White and suddenly Black's position is very passive.} 6...Nfd7 7.h4 {White tries to trap our bishop with h4, aiming for h5. Let's trade off the knight first.} 7...Nxe5 8.dxe5 {White recaptures...} 8...h5 {And after ...h5 we get to keep our bishop pair and aim at White's overextended kingside pawns which could prove to be a weakness in the future. We've untangled ourselves out of this situation.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Blackmar-Diemer Gambit and G4 Gambit / Study 1: White Plays Gambits With 2.Nc3 or 2.g4  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/39ec6fc0-3f96-4544-956f-7faf9cc741b1/studies/7fc43b70-f932-4141-9a1f-9d6989e2f63f/lines"]

1.d4 {For this last chapter, I want to teach you some sound lines against two White Gambits: The Blackmar-Diemer and the 2.g4 gambit.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {This looks like White could go for a Jobava London. We play d5 and establish central presence. This makes moves like e4 harder to play...} 2...d5 3.e4 {But if White plays 3.e4 anyways, this leads to the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit. 3.e4 is played about 10% of the time. We'll of course take. This is already slightly better for Black if played correctly.} 3...dxe4 4.f3 {60% of games continue like this. White offers a second pawn for quicker development and to get rid of our important e4-pawn. Even though White gets some activity, there are clever ways to defend and keep a safe position, so we'll take.} 4...exf3 5.Nxf3 {5.Nxf3 is the most popular recapture. The knight is a dangerous piece. It can jump to e5 or g5 and attack our f7-pawn. I suggest 5...g6 here to fianchetto. But another benefit of this move is that it blocks the h7-pawn so White won't be able to attack our kingside with Bd3.} 5...g6 6.Bc4 {The next best square for White's bishop is c4. It puts pressure on f7. We fianchetto.} 6...Bg7 7.O-O {White castles. Here, a benefit of 4.f3 is unveiled: their rook becomes active on the f-file. Castling helps us add another defender to f7.} 7...O-O 8.Bg5 {White attacks our knight. Because we have a pawn on e7, this move is not effective. 8...Bg4, on the other hand, does pin the knight on f3. We undermine the defense of d4 and threaten to exchange pieces.} 8...Bg4 9.Qd2 {White creates a queen and bishop battery. We have an attack on d4.} 9...Nc6 10.Rad1 {White defends.} 10...Qd6 {We have an idea: ...Bxf3 and ...Rad8 to put pressure on d4. White could play 11.Nb5, but we have 11...Qd7. Our middlegame plan is to continue attacking d4 and to trade pieces whenever possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Blackmar-Diemer Gambit and G4 Gambit / Study 1: White Plays Gambits With 2.Nc3 or 2.g4  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/39ec6fc0-3f96-4544-956f-7faf9cc741b1/studies/7fc43b70-f932-4141-9a1f-9d6989e2f63f/lines"]

1.d4 {For this last chapter, I want to teach you some sound lines against two White Gambits: The Blackmar-Diemer and the 2.g4 gambit.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {This looks like White could go for a Jobava London. We play d5 and establish central presence. This makes moves like e4 harder to play...} 2...d5 3.e4 {But if White plays 3.e4 anyways, this leads to the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit. 3.e4 is played about 10% of the time. We'll of course take. This is already slightly better for Black if played correctly.} 3...dxe4 4.f3 {60% of games continue like this. White offers a second pawn for quicker development and to get rid of our important e4-pawn. Even though White gets some activity, there are clever ways to defend and keep a safe position, so we'll take.} 4...exf3 5.Nxf3 {5.Nxf3 is the most popular recapture. The knight is a dangerous piece. It can jump to e5 or g5 and attack our f7-pawn. I suggest 5...g6 here to fianchetto. But another benefit of this move is that it blocks the h7-pawn so White won't be able to attack our kingside with Bd3.} 5...g6 6.Bc4 {The next best square for White's bishop is c4. It puts pressure on f7. We fianchetto.} 6...Bg7 7.O-O {White castles. Here, a benefit of 4.f3 is unveiled: their rook becomes active on the f-file. Castling helps us add another defender to f7.} 7...O-O 8.Qe1 {The queen goes to e1. The idea is to transfer to h4 and attack our kingside. Our idea is to undermine d4. The threat is ...Bxf3 and ...Qxd4+, winning the bishop on c4.} 8...Bg4 9.Qh4 {The queen indirectly guards d4. White prepares the idea of Ng5 to put pressure on h7. We here add another attacker on d4. The good thing is that Ng5 does not work because it drops d4 with a fork.} 9...Nc6 10.Be3 {White defends the pawn on d4. The fact that they're busy defending is a good sign: we've neutralized the gambit. 10...e6 makes our f7-pawn safer, and we also get the e7-square to maneuver.} 10...e6 11.Ne5 {11.Ng5 h6 is winning for us. White does not have an attack. 11.Ne5 attacks our bishop on g4.} 11...Bf5 {But we can simply go to f5. 12.g4 does not work. 12...Nxg4 is the right response because the queen on h4 is not protected. Our plan is to trade more pieces and convert our material advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Blackmar-Diemer Gambit and G4 Gambit / Study 1: White Plays Gambits With 2.Nc3 or 2.g4  / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/39ec6fc0-3f96-4544-956f-7faf9cc741b1/studies/7fc43b70-f932-4141-9a1f-9d6989e2f63f/lines"]

1.d4 {For this last chapter, I want to teach you some sound lines against two White Gambits: The Blackmar-Diemer and the 2.g4 gambit.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {This looks like White could go for a Jobava London. We play d5 and establish central presence. This makes moves like e4 harder to play...} 2...d5 3.e4 {But if White plays 3.e4 anyways, this leads to the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit. 3.e4 is played about 10% of the time. We'll of course take. This is already slightly better for Black if played correctly.} 3...dxe4 4.Bg5 {18% of games go like this. The purpose of 4.Bg5 is to undermine the defense of e4. White wants to develop while trying to get back the pawn. We can develop and defend e4.} 4...Bf5 5.Qe2 {White attacks the e4-pawn. Again, we don't want to take d4 because of Qb5+ ideas. So, we prevent Qb5+. If 6.Bxf6, we're happy to see that move because we get the bishop pair.} 5...c6 6.O-O-O {White castles. We develop the queenside knight and support the one on f6. White cannot win the pawn.} 6...Nbd7 7.f3 {White offers a pawn to get rid of our e4-pawn. We will accept it at this point.} 7...exf3 8.Nxf3 {White recaptures.} 8...Qa5 {And we develop our queen to a5. We will consolidate with ...e6, ...h6, ...Bb4 and ...Bh7. White has a tough road ahead if they want to prove compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Blackmar-Diemer Gambit and G4 Gambit / Study 1: White Plays Gambits With 2.Nc3 or 2.g4  / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/39ec6fc0-3f96-4544-956f-7faf9cc741b1/studies/7fc43b70-f932-4141-9a1f-9d6989e2f63f/lines"]

1.d4 {For this last chapter, I want to teach you some sound lines against two White Gambits: The Blackmar-Diemer and the 2.g4 gambit.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {This looks like White could go for a Jobava London. We play d5 and establish central presence. This makes moves like e4 harder to play...} 2...d5 3.e4 {But if White plays 3.e4 anyways, this leads to the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit. 3.e4 is played about 10% of the time. We'll of course take. This is already slightly better for Black if played correctly.} 3...dxe4 4.Bg5 {18% of games go like this. The purpose of 4.Bg5 is to undermine the defense of e4. White wants to develop while trying to get back the pawn. We can develop and defend e4.} 4...Bf5 5.f3 {White loses all hopes of capturing on e4. Instead, they offer up the f-pawn trade to make us voluntarily clear the center. Since we have two pieces defending e4, we can simply develop.} 5...Nbd7 6.fxe4 {White trades. We capture on e4. 7.Nxe4 Nxe4 gives us a centralized knight with a tempo on the bishop on g5.} 6...Bxe4 7.Nf3 {White develops their other knight to f3. We cover the b5- and d5-squares. A bit of consolidation once we've secured the extra pawn.} 7...c6 8.Bc4 {White develops their bishop, putting pressure on f7. We fall back and defend it.} 8...Bg6 9.O-O {White castles, with a powerful rook coming into the game.} 9...e6 {But we block the c4-bishop and get ready to play ...Be7 to castle and defend our king. We've made sure to play things safe, which is exactly what we must do. Now it's a matter of playing up a pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Blackmar-Diemer Gambit and G4 Gambit / Study 1: White Plays Gambits With 2.Nc3 or 2.g4  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/39ec6fc0-3f96-4544-956f-7faf9cc741b1/studies/7fc43b70-f932-4141-9a1f-9d6989e2f63f/lines"]

1.d4 {For this last chapter, I want to teach you some sound lines against two White Gambits: The Blackmar-Diemer and the 2.g4 gambit.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {This looks like White could go for a Jobava London. We play d5 and establish central presence. This makes moves like e4 harder to play...} 2...d5 3.e4 {But if White plays 3.e4 anyways, this leads to the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit. 3.e4 is played about 10% of the time. We'll of course take. This is already slightly better for Black if played correctly.} 3...dxe4 4.Bg5 {18% of games go like this. The purpose of 4.Bg5 is to undermine the defense of e4. White wants to develop while trying to get back the pawn. We can develop and defend e4.} 4...Bf5 5.Bxf6 {This is the most common move. The idea is to double our pawns and undermine the defense of e4. We'll recapture away from the center so that our f8-bishop can come into play.} 5...exf6 6.Qe2 {White attacks e4 and leaves d4 hanging. We don't want to go pawn hunting, as Qb5+ causes more problems if our queen isn't on d8. Instead, we want to develop.} 6...Be7 7.Qb5+ {Check! We have to block with our bishop.} 7...Bd7 8.Qxb7 {White takes on b7, winning a pawn back.} 8...Nc6 {But the pawn on b7 isn't as valuable as a center pawn. We're attacking d4 and ...Rb1 is coming. White won't be able to defend against all the threats and Black is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Blackmar-Diemer Gambit and G4 Gambit / Study 1: White Plays Gambits With 2.Nc3 or 2.g4  / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/39ec6fc0-3f96-4544-956f-7faf9cc741b1/studies/7fc43b70-f932-4141-9a1f-9d6989e2f63f/lines"]

1.d4 {For this last chapter, I want to teach you some sound lines against two White Gambits: The Blackmar-Diemer and the 2.g4 gambit.} 1...Nf6 2.g4 {This is technically called the Gibbins-Weidenhagen Gambit, but you don't need to remember such a lofty name for this opening. The important thing is that it's less than 1% of games, and Black gets a small advantage. White wants to give up the g4-pawn to get strong central control. We'll take.} 2...Nxg4 3.e4 {White wants to occupy the center and attack our knight. But we can challenge their center and defend our knight with 3...d5. This variation already nearly refutes their gambit. 4.e5 Bf5 is good for us.} 3...d5 4.Be2 {If they develop and attack, we must retreat. We here attack e4. The move e5 isn't that big of a threat because White lacks a follow-up.} 4...Nf6 5.e5 {Our knight is attacked and forced to a worse square. But these positions are only good for White if they have a concrete line to gain an advantage. Their development of one piece is not enough compensation for them.} 5...Ng8 6.Nc3 {White develops and puts pressure on d5. But because their pawns are on d4 and e5, we get the f5-square for our bishop like in the advanced Caro-Kann.} 6...Bf5 7.Nf3 {They develop their knight.} 7...e6 {But we're easily consolidating. A simple but effective plan against this Nc3 setup is ...Nc6 and ...Nb4 to attack c2.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Blackmar-Diemer Gambit and G4 Gambit / Study 1: White Plays Gambits With 2.Nc3 or 2.g4  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5a2a0988-2a87-4d59-8360-7228ded553de/chapters/39ec6fc0-3f96-4544-956f-7faf9cc741b1/studies/7fc43b70-f932-4141-9a1f-9d6989e2f63f/lines"]

1.d4 {For this last chapter, I want to teach you some sound lines against two White Gambits: The Blackmar-Diemer and the 2.g4 gambit.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {This looks like White could go for a Jobava London. We play d5 and establish central presence. This makes moves like e4 harder to play...} 2...d5 3.e4 {But if White plays 3.e4 anyways, this leads to the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit. 3.e4 is played about 10% of the time. We'll of course take. This is already slightly better for Black if played correctly.} 3...dxe4 4.f3 {60% of games continue like this. White offers a second pawn for quicker development and to get rid of our important e4-pawn. Even though White gets some activity, there are clever ways to defend and keep a safe position, so we'll take.} 4...exf3 5.Qxf3 {5.Qxf3 is nearly -2 according to the engine. It instantly puts pressure on b7. But the queen defended something important... That's two pawns, which is why the engine gave this position a -2 evaluation in our favor.} 5...Qxd4 6.Be3 {White develops with a tempo, but fortunately we can move away with a tempo as well. Offering queen trades gives us tempi because White does not want to trade queens down material. 7.O-O-O is popular, but we can simply trade queens.} 6...Qg4 7.Qf2 {White avoids the trade. We offer the trade once more. We should annoy White as much as we can.} 7...Qf5 8.Nf3 {The knight is developed. They block the queen trade, but we can trade something else. This is a fork. We will trade our knight for the bishop.} 8...Ng4 9.Qe2 {The queen moves away. We trade. This is exactly what we want because we are up material.} 9...Nxe3 10.Qxe3 {White recaptures. Let us briefly evaluate the position. We are up two pawns and have the bishop pair. I rest my case. We play 10...e6 to develop our dark-squared bishop.} 10...e6 11.O-O-O {White castles.} 11...Bd6 {Converting this game should be easy. We will develop with moves like ...Nc6 and ...O-O. We have a massive threat of ...Bf4. If 12.Nd5, 12...h5 helps us prevent 13.g4, since White might want g4 and Rg1.} *